WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:02.202
[music playing]

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:04.471
- They're all live oysters?

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:05.805
- This is all live oysters.

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:07.173
- So they're everywhere.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.642
I'm here in Maine or
North Haven island, where

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:12.746
I'm going to harvest oysters
with Adam, a local farmer

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:16.416
of America's favorite mollusk.

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:18.118
- This little, tiny
bed can produce

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.453
250 to 300,000 oysters a year.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.255
- I'm walking through
the water, thinking,

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:23.390
where are these things.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.891
I'm actually standing on them.

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:26.693
It was a proper oyster bed.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.627
They're free.

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:28.728
Nothing's caged.

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:29.829
Nothing's in racks.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:30.730
Look at size of this.

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:31.898
- Oh, it's a monsters

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:32.899
- That is insane.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.371
What an amazing little river.

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:41.141
- So the freshwater comes
from freshwater pond up here.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:42.409
- Yeah.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:43.576
- And then the tide
comes from the sea.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.311
And then the two
meat, and it creates

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:46.613
really good algae blooms.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:47.714
- Yeah.

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:49.516
That's the advantage
of the tides obviously.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:50.216
- And the flow.

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:51.251
- And the flow.

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:52.352
- And so that's gorgeous one.

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:54.521
- That's a beauty there.

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.891
The extreme tidal fluctuations
of this estuary and the mixing

00:00:57.957 --> 00:00:59.726
of fresh and salt
water are a part

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:02.295
of what makes this the
perfect environment

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.597
to grow the oysters.

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:06.032
How do they get here?

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:07.600
- I grew oysters in
the past four years,

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.436
and I have this kid
harvesting for me,

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.105
and he shucked the oyster
and dropped them on the bed.

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.640
And they created
a natural spawn.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:17.110
So I came down the next morning,
and the whole 17-acre pond

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:19.212
had three feet of foam on it.

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:21.848
And I was like, oh, my
God, what's going on?

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.418
- Some of the oyster larvae swam
into this connected estuary,

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:28.354
where they ended up thriving
on the gravel bottom.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:33.126
So Adam now replicates this
process for each new batch.

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:34.360
- Actually, you want an oyster?

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:35.695
You want try one out right now?

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:36.229
- I'd love one now.

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:36.863
Yes, please.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:37.730
Yeah.

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:38.498
Are you kidding me?

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:39.065
- Let's do that.

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:40.033
- Look at that.

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.835
Oh, my God.

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:42.769
- Try that.

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:43.670
- Thank you.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.071
Just look at the color.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:46.439
My God.

00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:48.975
They're nice and thick,
and dense, creamy.

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:49.909
- Mm-hmm.

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:50.677
- Cheers.

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:51.177
Look at that.

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:51.878
- Cheers.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:52.545
- Cheers.

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.017
Bloody hell.

00:01:58.084 --> 00:01:59.219
Man--

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.553
- There's a reason
I have five kids.

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:04.557
- Guess how many kids I've got?

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:05.492
- How many?

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:06.059
- Five.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:06.826
- No [bleep].

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:07.927
That's awesome.

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:09.395
For me, it makes
a big difference

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.464
having these free-range,
bottom-cultured oysters,

00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:13.333
because they're spread out.

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:14.834
There's nothing set in cages.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.270
They're not hanging
horizontally, vertically.

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:20.807
They literally are laying
on the bed of that river,

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:24.444
and then this surge of
saltwater against the freshwater

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:26.880
is just producing
a perfect oyster.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:29.949
Honestly, some of the best
oysters I've ever eaten.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:30.884
Oh, my God.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:31.918
- All right.

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:33.620
Let's go smoke some big ones.

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:36.623
[music playing]

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.094
So these oysters here
are market oysters.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.529
So this like half shell market.

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:43.630
- Gotcha.

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:45.064
- The big ones like
this one we got today,

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:48.501
that is not a half shell oyster.

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.169
- How do you open
something like that?

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:51.070
- I'll shuck this one.

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:51.971
- Please.

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:53.406
- Check it out.

00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:54.340
Oh.

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:56.643
- Man, look at that.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:57.777
I mean, honestly.

00:02:57.844 --> 00:02:59.178
Wow.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.613
- So then we'll brine
that overnight, and then--

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.614
- That is beautiful.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.016
So beautiful.

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.984
- And then this is what it looks
like after 24 hours of smoking.

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:05.885
- Oh, my God.

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.020
- Right here.

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:08.121
- So they've
already been brined.

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:09.355
- They're brined, smoked.

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.089
- Wow.

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:12.158
- Yeah.

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:13.593
- Mm, that's delicious.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:14.661
- Yeah.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.195
And then the way we
finish them is in--

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:17.463
- There's another process?

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.299
- Yeah.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:22.402
We pack them in virgin
olive oil with a couple

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:24.237
other little secret ingredients.

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.605
- I love that there's secrecy.

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:27.140
- We've got to
have a few secrets.

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:28.041
- That smells sweet.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:28.875
Is there honey in there?

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:29.509
- Yeah, there is.

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:30.443
See.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.144
You got me.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.412
[bleep] You got me.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.113
- Chefs share their
secrets, right?

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:35.048
- Yeah.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:36.215
One's enough.

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:39.819
- Mm, my God.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:40.887
Wow.

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:42.121
That is so good.

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:43.456
Smoked oysters are out there.

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:45.291
You know, it's a fine
balance when something's

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:46.793
